---
title: "OAuth clients"
description: "Manage the OAuth applications your organization owns and their client secrets."
---

OAuth clients are applications owned by an organization. They use the
[selected organization](/cli/organizations#select-an-organization). Applications
*you* have authorized are account connections, listed under
[`photon account oauth`](/cli/accounts/oauth).

```text
photon org oauth-client
├── list
├── view <client-id>
├── create --name <name> --redirect-uri <uri>...
│          --default-redirect-uri <uri>
│          [--description <text>] [--scope <scope>...]
│          [--idempotency-key <key>]
├── update <client-id> [configuration options]
├── authorize <client-id> [--redirect-uri <uri>] [--scope <scope>...]
│                         [--no-browser]
├── delete <client-id> [--yes]
└── secret
    ├── view <client-id>
    ├── create <client-id> [--idempotency-key <key>]
    ├── rotate <client-id> [--emergency] [--yes] [--idempotency-key <key>]
    └── revoke <client-id> [--yes]
```

## Clients

```sh
photon org oauth-client list
photon org oauth-client view client_123

photon org oauth-client create \
  --name "Acme Integration" \
  --redirect-uri https://acme.example/callback \
  --default-redirect-uri https://acme.example/callback \
  --scope account:read \
  --scope project:read
```

`--redirect-uri` and `--scope` are repeatable; without `--scope` the client has
no delegated scopes. The default redirect must exactly match one of the
supplied redirect URIs. Redirect URIs must use HTTPS, except for HTTP loopback
URLs during development. User info, fragments, duplicates, and more than ten
redirect URIs are rejected before the request is sent.

Creation sends an idempotency key, generated unless you pass
`--idempotency-key`. If the outcome is uncertain — the connection dropped or
Photon answered with a server error — the error names the key: retry the same
command with it rather than a new one, and Photon returns the client the first
request created.

`update` replaces redirect URIs or scopes when you supply them. Use
`--clear-description` or `--clear-scopes` to remove those values:

```sh
photon org oauth-client update client_123 --name "New name"
photon org oauth-client update client_123 \
  --redirect-uri https://new.example/callback \
  --default-redirect-uri https://new.example/callback
photon org oauth-client delete client_123 --yes
```

`delete` reports `status`. When Photon has accepted the deletion but provider
cleanup is still pending, the CLI warns that existing tokens may keep working
until it completes.

## Test an authorization

`authorize` builds an authorization-code request and opens it, which is the
fastest way to check a client's redirect URIs and scopes:

```sh
photon org oauth-client authorize client_123

photon org oauth-client authorize client_123 \
  --redirect-uri http://localhost:3000/callback \
  --scope account:read \
  --no-browser
```

It reads the scope catalogue, discovers the authorization server through
Photon's protected-resource metadata, reads the client, and validates the
redirect URI and scopes before opening anything. With the options omitted, it
uses the client's default redirect URI and configured scopes.

<Note>
  This helper only generates the authorization URL, state, and nonce. Your
  registered application handles the callback and exchanges the code with its
  own client secret — the CLI never accepts or stores that secret.
</Note>

## Secrets

An OAuth client has one secret. Rotating it replaces it with a new one, and the
replaced secret keeps working for one hour so your application can switch over.

```sh
photon org oauth-client secret view client_123
photon org oauth-client secret create client_123
photon org oauth-client secret rotate client_123 --yes
photon org oauth-client secret rotate client_123 --emergency --yes
photon org oauth-client secret revoke client_123 --yes
```

- `secret view` returns metadata only: the current secret's hint, creation
  time and last use, and the replaced secret with `validUntil` while it still
  works.
- `secret create` sets up the client's first secret. If the client already has
  one, it fails with `OAUTH_SECRET_CONFIGURED`; replace it with `secret rotate`.
- `secret rotate` mints a new secret. The replaced secret works until
  `previousSecretValidUntil`, one hour from now; with `--emergency` it stops
  now and `previousSecretValidUntil` is `null`. A client with no secret fails
  with `OAUTH_SECRET_NOT_CONFIGURED`; set one up with `secret create`.
- `secret revoke` stops the secret now, and a replaced secret still in its
  hour stops with it.

```console
$ photon org oauth-client secret rotate client_123 --yes --json
{"previousSecretValidUntil":"2026-11-02T19:00:00.000Z","value":"..."}
```

<Warning>
  `secret create` and `secret rotate` print the plaintext secret **once**. Save
  it immediately. Photon never stores it locally, and `--debug` output redacts
  the response.

  Creating and rotating a secret are not journaled, and the plaintext is never
  stored. After an uncertain failure, retry with the `--idempotency-key` the
  error names: Photon then reports the earlier attempt's status instead of
  minting a second secret, and the CLI reports `OAUTH_SECRET_NOT_DELIVERED`.
  Plaintext is only returned to the request that minted the secret, so if it
  was lost, rotate again.
</Warning>

## JSON output

With `--json`, each command prints the API's response as Photon sends it, with
every field in it, including a field the API adds after this CLI release:

| Command | JSON |
| --- | --- |
| `list` | `{"oauthClients": [...]}` |
| `view`, `create`, `update` | The client: `{"clientId": "...", "name": "...", "description": ..., "redirectUris": [...], "scopes": [...], "createdAt": "...", "updatedAt": "..."}` |
| `delete` | `{"operationId": "...", "status": "deleted"}`, with `"status": "cleanup_required"` while provider cleanup is pending |
| `secret view` | `{"current": {...}, "previous": {...}}`, each `null` when there is no such secret |
| `secret create` | `{"value": "..."}`, the plaintext secret |
| `secret rotate` | `{"previousSecretValidUntil": "...", "value": "..."}` |

`update` reads the client for its ETag before it changes it, and prints the
response to the change.

Two commands print a document the CLI defines instead. `authorize` builds the
authorization request itself, with a state and nonce of its own, and
`secret revoke` gets no body from Photon, so it names what it revoked:

| Command | JSON |
| --- | --- |
| `authorize` | `{"authorization": {"authorizationUrl": "...", "clientId": "...", "issuer": "...", "nonce": "...", "opened": true, "redirectUri": "...", "scopes": [...], "state": "..."}}`, with `"opened": false` after `--no-browser` or when the browser could not be opened |
| `secret revoke` | `{"clientId": "...", "revoked": true}` |

<Note>
  These commands replaced `photon account oauth client` and
  `photon account oauth secret`, which now only point here. `secret list`
  became `secret view`, since a client has one secret.
</Note>
